1
00:00:00,758 --> 00:00:07,638
I want to be long-winded and unclear.

2
00:00:07,638 --> 00:00:09,858
It's not a common opening line.

3
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:13,498
Most people won't tell us when they're going to be long-winded,

4
00:00:13,498 --> 00:00:15,258
but we know.

5
00:00:15,258 --> 00:00:19,498
We can tell pretty quickly if someone’s going to get to the point

6
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:24,578
or if we are in for a long and painful conversation.

7
00:00:24,578 --> 00:00:28,378
And this is especially true at work.

8
00:00:28,378 --> 00:00:33,778
Every year, poor communication costs businesses hundreds of billions of dollars

9
00:00:33,778 --> 00:00:35,598
in lost productivity.

10
00:00:35,598 --> 00:00:39,618
And I have to confess, [in] the early part of my career,

11
00:00:39,618 --> 00:00:43,878
I was responsible for at least some of that.

12
00:00:43,878 --> 00:00:45,758
But I'm not alone.

13
00:00:45,758 --> 00:00:51,298
Hands up if you work with someone who is long winded or unclear.

14
00:00:51,298 --> 00:00:53,518
That's a lot of hands.

15
00:00:53,518 --> 00:00:58,358
Now I’m sure we are all thinking about people outside this room, right?

16
00:00:58,358 --> 00:01:01,718
Except for those of you pointing at each other.

17
00:01:01,718 --> 00:01:06,278
But what would happen if we asked the people outside this room?

18
00:01:06,278 --> 00:01:10,038
They might be thinking about us.

19
00:01:10,038 --> 00:01:12,958
And while our goal is to be clear and concise,

20
00:01:12,958 --> 00:01:16,438
do we really know how?

21
00:01:16,438 --> 00:01:18,418
Every time we communicate,

22
00:01:18,418 --> 00:01:22,358
we have an opportunity to create clarity

23
00:01:22,358 --> 00:01:24,618
or confusion.

24
00:01:24,618 --> 00:01:29,678
And clarity begins from the very first minute.

25
00:01:29,678 --> 00:01:31,318
If you get the first minute right,

26
00:01:31,318 --> 00:01:35,338
you set your conversation up for success.

27
00:01:35,338 --> 00:01:36,818
Get it wrong [and]

28
00:01:36,818 --> 00:01:40,358
your conversations will be longer, less effective

29
00:01:40,358 --> 00:01:44,758
and more frustrating.

30
00:01:44,758 --> 00:01:51,098
Now on the off chance we could all use a little more clarity,

31
00:01:51,098 --> 00:01:53,418
I'm going to share a tip with you today.

32
00:01:53,418 --> 00:01:57,278
A tip that will help each of you improve the success

33
00:01:57,278 --> 00:01:59,738
of your communication at work.

34
00:01:59,738 --> 00:02:04,718
A tip that has been tested by more than 75,000 people around the world.

35
00:02:04,718 --> 00:02:09,398
And a tip that will help each of you get the first minute right.

36
00:02:09,398 --> 00:02:10,618
And here it is.

37
00:02:10,618 --> 00:02:13,518
There are three things we must include

38
00:02:13,518 --> 00:02:16,678
at the start of our work messages.

39
00:02:16,678 --> 00:02:20,018
And those three things begin with T,

40
00:02:20,018 --> 00:02:22,978
I and P.

41
00:02:22,978 --> 00:02:25,318
It's a TIP.

42
00:02:25,318 --> 00:02:27,438
Now the first part of the tip is the T,

43
00:02:27,438 --> 00:02:30,358
and T stands for topic.

44
00:02:30,358 --> 00:02:32,958
Every conversation at work needs a clear topic.

45
00:02:32,958 --> 00:02:34,958
When the topic is clear,

46
00:02:34,958 --> 00:02:36,918
everyone knows what we're talking about.

47
00:02:36,918 --> 00:02:38,198
Everyone's in sync.

48
00:02:38,198 --> 00:02:41,038
Everybody understands.

49
00:02:41,038 --> 00:02:45,638
Unfortunately, reality isn't always like this, is it?

50
00:02:45,638 --> 00:02:49,518
How often are you in a conversation and you find yourself thinking,

51
00:02:49,518 --> 00:02:51,498
what are you talking about?

52
00:02:51,498 --> 00:02:52,978
(Laughter)

53
00:02:52,978 --> 00:02:57,238
That happens when the topic is not clear.

54
00:02:57,238 --> 00:02:59,598
Now usually the consequences are pretty minor --

55
00:02:59,598 --> 00:03:02,598
a little bit of wasted time, some minor frustration --

56
00:03:02,598 --> 00:03:08,578
but sometimes, the consequences are more significant.

57
00:03:08,578 --> 00:03:10,078
In 2006,

58
00:03:10,078 --> 00:03:12,778
I was part of an engineering team writing a bid --

59
00:03:12,778 --> 00:03:16,078
a proposal -- that was worth a lot of money to our company.

60
00:03:16,078 --> 00:03:18,158
And a couple of days before the big meeting,

61
00:03:18,158 --> 00:03:19,658
my boss came up to me and said,

62
00:03:19,658 --> 00:03:23,138
"Hey, Chris, I need you to change the meeting from Tuesday to Thursday."

63
00:03:23,138 --> 00:03:25,478
So off I went and I changed the meeting.

64
00:03:25,478 --> 00:03:27,718
I typed an email, sent it to the client saying,

65
00:03:27,718 --> 00:03:29,758
"Something’s come up, we've got to change."

66
00:03:29,758 --> 00:03:33,238
And about a minute after I hit send, my phone rings.

67
00:03:33,238 --> 00:03:34,678
It's my boss.

68
00:03:34,678 --> 00:03:38,558
He had one question:

69
00:03:38,558 --> 00:03:42,198
"Why did you change the meeting?"

70
00:03:42,198 --> 00:03:45,918
You see, he was talking about our one-to-one,

71
00:03:45,918 --> 00:03:48,758
and I was thinking about the client meeting.

72
00:03:48,758 --> 00:03:50,178
The lack of a clear topic

73
00:03:50,178 --> 00:03:55,778
meant he and I were thinking about two very different things.

74
00:03:55,778 --> 00:03:58,618
Now that might be the single biggest contribution I’ve made

75
00:03:58,618 --> 00:04:00,678
to the cost of bad communication,

76
00:04:00,678 --> 00:04:02,538
but it also marks the moment

77
00:04:02,538 --> 00:04:08,218
at which I began to be interested in improving how we communicate at work.

78
00:04:08,218 --> 00:04:11,158
So all our messages have to begin with a clear topic.

79
00:04:11,158 --> 00:04:13,198
But how? How do we do this?

80
00:04:13,198 --> 00:04:14,958
It's really easy.

81
00:04:14,958 --> 00:04:17,158
Start your message with one sentence

82
00:04:17,158 --> 00:04:20,518
that names the topic you want to talk about,

83
00:04:20,518 --> 00:04:23,118
and the more specific, the better.

84
00:04:23,118 --> 00:04:26,098
You could say, "I'd like to talk about July sales figures."

85
00:04:26,098 --> 00:04:29,078
"I want to talk about Project Everest's timeline."

86
00:04:29,078 --> 00:04:32,918
Or "Can I talk about our one-to-one this week?"

87
00:04:32,918 --> 00:04:35,078
One message, one sentence

88
00:04:35,078 --> 00:04:37,298
that lets your audience know what the topic is.

89
00:04:37,298 --> 00:04:38,818
They won't be thinking,

90
00:04:38,818 --> 00:04:42,138
"What are you talking about?"

91
00:04:42,138 --> 00:04:44,658
Now having a clear topic is a great start,

92
00:04:44,658 --> 00:04:46,278
but it's not enough.

93
00:04:46,278 --> 00:04:49,478
We need the second part of the tip,

94
00:04:49,478 --> 00:04:50,838
which is the I.

95
00:04:50,838 --> 00:04:53,178
And I stands for intent.

96
00:04:53,178 --> 00:04:58,898
Intent is what we want the other person to do with our message.

97
00:04:58,898 --> 00:05:02,058
Have you ever been in a conversation and a few minutes in thought,

98
00:05:02,058 --> 00:05:04,998
"Why are you telling me this?"

99
00:05:04,998 --> 00:05:08,618
That happens when the intent isn't clear,

100
00:05:08,618 --> 00:05:11,018
when the speaker hasn't told you what to do

101
00:05:11,018 --> 00:05:14,738
with the information that they are giving you.

102
00:05:14,738 --> 00:05:18,898
Now there’s a process in our brains called selective attention,

103
00:05:18,898 --> 00:05:23,078
and this filters incoming information and tells you what is important

104
00:05:23,078 --> 00:05:24,938
and what is not.

105
00:05:24,938 --> 00:05:27,218
And if you don't know what to do with a message,

106
00:05:27,218 --> 00:05:29,778
your brain says, not important.

107
00:05:29,778 --> 00:05:32,878
And we stop listening.

108
00:05:32,878 --> 00:05:34,338
At the beginning of this talk,

109
00:05:34,338 --> 00:05:36,298
I told you I would give you a tip

110
00:05:36,298 --> 00:05:39,458
that would help improve the success of your communication at work.

111
00:05:39,458 --> 00:05:43,118
That told your brains what to do with this information.

112
00:05:43,118 --> 00:05:44,318
If I hadn't done that,

113
00:05:44,318 --> 00:05:47,478
I could be faced with a room full of people smiling politely

114
00:05:47,478 --> 00:05:51,938
while all thinking, "Why are you telling us this?"

115
00:05:51,938 --> 00:05:54,378
Now that is something we want to avoid at work.

116
00:05:54,378 --> 00:05:55,758
But there is another,

117
00:05:55,758 --> 00:06:00,258
and perhaps more important reason to make your intent clear.

118
00:06:00,258 --> 00:06:05,378
You want to avoid frustrating the people you're talking to.

119
00:06:05,378 --> 00:06:07,558
I'd had a particularly bad day,

120
00:06:07,558 --> 00:06:09,698
and I walked into my colleague Tina's office,

121
00:06:09,698 --> 00:06:11,718
and I sat down with my head in my hands,

122
00:06:11,718 --> 00:06:16,678
and I told her how I'd messed up on an important call with a senior leader.

123
00:06:16,678 --> 00:06:20,018
And when I finished talking, Tina very, very helpfully said,

124
00:06:20,018 --> 00:06:23,218
"Oh, why don't you try this and maybe we can avoid this next time."

125
00:06:23,218 --> 00:06:26,398
And she was giving me all sorts of advice.

126
00:06:26,398 --> 00:06:28,338
The more she spoke,

127
00:06:28,338 --> 00:06:31,338
the more frustrated I became.

128
00:06:31,338 --> 00:06:33,218
I didn't want her help.

129
00:06:33,218 --> 00:06:35,018
And that must have been quite obvious

130
00:06:35,018 --> 00:06:37,058
because she just put up her hands and said,

131
00:06:37,058 --> 00:06:38,638
"You clearly don't want my help.

132
00:06:38,638 --> 00:06:40,138
So why are you here?"

133
00:06:40,138 --> 00:06:41,698
I said, "Tina, I'm really sorry.

134
00:06:41,698 --> 00:06:43,558
I just want to get this off my chest.

135
00:06:43,558 --> 00:06:45,778
Can I just talk for a minute?"

136
00:06:45,778 --> 00:06:47,998
And like a mother talking to a small child,

137
00:06:47,998 --> 00:06:49,438
she rolled her eyes and said,

138
00:06:49,438 --> 00:06:53,158
"Well, why didn't you start with that?"

139
00:06:53,158 --> 00:06:57,518
We need to tell our audience what to do with the information we're giving them.

140
00:06:57,518 --> 00:07:01,458
Tina was working hard from the moment I started talking.

141
00:07:01,458 --> 00:07:02,638
And all the way through,

142
00:07:02,638 --> 00:07:05,638
she was working hard to help me and I didn't want or need that.

143
00:07:05,638 --> 00:07:08,158
She was annoyed and rightly so.

144
00:07:08,158 --> 00:07:11,298
So when you start your message, after you've given a clear topic,

145
00:07:11,298 --> 00:07:13,138
tell your audience what to do with it.

146
00:07:13,138 --> 00:07:15,418
And again, you can do this with one line.

147
00:07:15,418 --> 00:07:16,918
Make your intent clear.

148
00:07:16,918 --> 00:07:19,058
If you have a question, say,

149
00:07:19,058 --> 00:07:21,018
"Can I ask you a question?"

150
00:07:21,018 --> 00:07:23,618
If you want someone to make a decision, say,

151
00:07:23,618 --> 00:07:26,318
"I'd like your help with this decision."

152
00:07:26,318 --> 00:07:29,418
If you want to get something off your chest, just say,

153
00:07:29,418 --> 00:07:32,498
"Is it OK if I vent for a minute?"

154
00:07:32,498 --> 00:07:35,118
If you tell your audience what to do with your message,

155
00:07:35,118 --> 00:07:37,298
not only will their brains pay more attention,

156
00:07:37,298 --> 00:07:40,178
you will avoid frustrating them.

157
00:07:40,178 --> 00:07:42,938
That's kind of useful.

158
00:07:42,938 --> 00:07:45,138
So we've made our topic clear.

159
00:07:45,138 --> 00:07:47,878
We've told our audience what we intend for them to do.

160
00:07:47,878 --> 00:07:50,018
It's time to get down into the detail, right?

161
00:07:50,018 --> 00:07:51,858
Get into the message.

162
00:07:51,858 --> 00:07:53,538
Wrong.

163
00:07:53,538 --> 00:07:57,278
This is where we need the third part of the tip.

164
00:07:57,278 --> 00:07:58,518
The P.

165
00:07:58,518 --> 00:08:00,778
And P stands for point.

166
00:08:00,778 --> 00:08:04,918
Point is the most important part of our message.

167
00:08:04,918 --> 00:08:07,998
If you only had five seconds to speak,

168
00:08:07,998 --> 00:08:09,998
it's what you would say.

169
00:08:09,998 --> 00:08:15,918
And usually it's what your audience cares about the most.

170
00:08:15,918 --> 00:08:18,278
When I was a kid, my dad played a game with me.

171
00:08:18,278 --> 00:08:21,398
If we were going on a journey, I'd go, "Dad, where are we going?"

172
00:08:21,398 --> 00:08:23,738
And he'd say, "Well, first, we're going to the car

173
00:08:23,738 --> 00:08:26,078
and then we’ll get in the car, leave the driveway,

174
00:08:26,078 --> 00:08:29,078
turn left onto the main street until we get to the post office,

175
00:08:29,078 --> 00:08:31,818
and then turn right until we go to a big yellow building."

176
00:08:31,818 --> 00:08:34,378
I just wanted to scream, "Where are we going?"

177
00:08:34,378 --> 00:08:36,218
I wanted the short version.

178
00:08:36,218 --> 00:08:39,098
I think he just wanted to wind me up a little bit.

179
00:08:39,098 --> 00:08:41,978
But fast forward a few years,

180
00:08:41,978 --> 00:08:45,418
I was doing the same thing at work.

181
00:08:45,418 --> 00:08:46,978
Someone would ask me a question,

182
00:08:46,978 --> 00:08:51,318
or I’d start a conversation, and I would give all the detail,

183
00:08:51,318 --> 00:08:54,138
step by step information.

184
00:08:54,138 --> 00:08:55,938
And every so often --

185
00:08:55,938 --> 00:08:57,578
OK, quite often --

186
00:08:57,578 --> 00:09:02,398
people would say, "Chris, just get to the point."

187
00:09:02,398 --> 00:09:03,658
And that's what we need.

188
00:09:03,658 --> 00:09:05,518
We need a point, we need a headline,

189
00:09:05,518 --> 00:09:09,758
something that tells us where this conversation is going.

190
00:09:09,758 --> 00:09:13,018
Now, of course, there's a lot more information we could include,

191
00:09:13,018 --> 00:09:14,378
but that comes later.

192
00:09:14,378 --> 00:09:17,498
That comes after we've set up the audience

193
00:09:17,498 --> 00:09:20,338
to understand what the conversation is about.

194
00:09:20,338 --> 00:09:23,318
And we can do this quite simply,

195
00:09:23,318 --> 00:09:24,738
as the military likes to say,

196
00:09:24,738 --> 00:09:27,958
“Put the bottom line up front.”

197
00:09:27,958 --> 00:09:29,178
In one sentence,

198
00:09:29,178 --> 00:09:31,998
deliver the most important piece of information.

199
00:09:31,998 --> 00:09:34,718
"I want to give a refund for a late delivery."

200
00:09:34,718 --> 00:09:37,358
"Our project launch is going to be delayed by a month."

201
00:09:37,358 --> 00:09:39,478
Say the most important thing,

202
00:09:39,478 --> 00:09:42,378
and your audience will know where that conversation is going

203
00:09:42,378 --> 00:09:47,578
and they won't be thinking, "What is your point?"

204
00:09:47,578 --> 00:09:52,018
Now if you start your conversation with topic, intent and point,

205
00:09:52,018 --> 00:09:55,458
you are starting with a clear and concise introduction,

206
00:09:55,458 --> 00:09:58,518
an introduction that answers the three questions we all have:

207
00:09:58,518 --> 00:10:00,258
What are you talking about?

208
00:10:00,258 --> 00:10:01,998
Why are you telling me this?

209
00:10:01,998 --> 00:10:04,538
And what is your point?

210
00:10:04,538 --> 00:10:06,678
Because here's the truth.

211
00:10:06,678 --> 00:10:08,538
Every time we communicate,

212
00:10:08,538 --> 00:10:11,578
it is an opportunity to create clarity

213
00:10:11,578 --> 00:10:13,558
or confusion.

214
00:10:13,558 --> 00:10:18,858
And topic, intent and point is clarity.

215
00:10:18,858 --> 00:10:20,978
So I have a challenge for you.

216
00:10:20,978 --> 00:10:23,618
The next time you start a conversation at work,

217
00:10:23,618 --> 00:10:25,418
the next time you write a message,

218
00:10:25,418 --> 00:10:28,958
take a moment and think to yourself,

219
00:10:28,958 --> 00:10:31,018
what is my topic?

220
00:10:31,018 --> 00:10:34,538
What do I intend for the other person to do with this message?

221
00:10:34,538 --> 00:10:38,678
And what point do I want to deliver?

222
00:10:38,678 --> 00:10:43,638
Imagine working somewhere where everybody did this.

223
00:10:43,638 --> 00:10:47,158
Imagine if everyone always knew what to do with the message you gave them,

224
00:10:47,158 --> 00:10:48,758
or understood what it was about.

225
00:10:48,758 --> 00:10:53,258
How much smoother, how much easier would our jobs be?

226
00:10:53,258 --> 00:10:55,418
That, ladies and gentlemen,

227
00:10:55,418 --> 00:10:59,218
is the power of the first minute.

228
00:10:59,218 --> 00:11:00,378
Thank you.

229
00:11:00,378 --> 00:11:04,378
(Applause)
